 \section{Discussion and limitations}
 This note is useful chiefly as an accounting identity. It translates a standard time-overhead law into energy-rate variables and makes model failure visible. It does not model multiple failures per interval, correlated faults, variable checkpoint size, restart latency, storage retention, cooling, communication, or useful-work slowdown. Daly's higher-order analysis is the relevant warning against treating Eq.~\eqref{eq:loss} as universal. The Landauer floor is many orders below typical device costs and becomes inapplicable as written when the implementation avoids the declared irreversible overwrite.
 
